      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the total number of ways to arrange 9 people around a round table and then determine the number of favorable arrangements where all four math majors sit in consecutive seats.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the total number of ways to arrange 9 people around a round table.**\n\nFor \\( n \\) people sitting in a circle, the number of ways to arrange them is given by \\((n-1)!\\). Here, \\( n = 9 \\):\n\n\\[\n(9-1)! = 8! = 40320\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the number of favorable arrangements where all four math majors sit in consecutive seats.**\n\nTreat the group of 4 math majors as a single \"block\". This block can be arranged among itself in \\( 4! \\) ways. After placing this block, we have 6 remaining seats to fill with the remaining 5 people (3 physics majors and 2 biology majors).\n\nThe number of ways to arrange these 6 people around a circle is \\((6-1)! = 5! = 120\\).\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the number of favorable arrangements.**\n\nThe number of favorable arrangements is the product of the number of ways to arrange the block of 4 math majors and the number of ways to arrange the remaining 6 people:\n\n\\[\n4! \\times 5! = 24 \\times 120 = 2880\n\\]\n\n**Step 4: Calculate the probability.**\n\nThe probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is the number of favorable arrangements divided by the total number of arrangements:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2880}{40320} = \\frac{1}{14}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\n\\]",
