      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats around a round table, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of ways to arrange 9 people around a round table.**\n   The number of ways to arrange \\( n \\) people around a round table is \\((n-1)!\\). For 9 people, this is:\n   \\[\n   (9-1)! = 8! = 40320\n   \\]\n\n2. **Treat the block of 4 consecutive math majors as a single \"super person\".**\n   This means we are now arranging 6 \"people\" around the table: the \"super person\" of 4 math majors and the 5 other individuals (3 physics majors and 2 biology majors). The number of ways to arrange these 6 \"people\" around a round table is:\n   \\[\n   (6-1)! = 5! = 120\n   \\]\n   Within the \"super person\", the 4 math majors can be arranged in \\(4!\\) ways. So, the number of ways to arrange the 4 math majors and the 5 other individuals is:\n   \\[\n   4! \\times 5! = 24 \\times 120 = 2880\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability.**\n   The probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is the number of favorable arrangements divided by the total number of arrangements. Therefore, the probability is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2880}{40320} = \\frac{1}{14}\n   \\]\n\nSo, the probability that all four math majors sit in consecutive seats is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{14}}\\).",
